  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Tiger Sawgill (Lentinus tigrinus) is a wood-decaying bracket fungus characterized by its distinctive toothed underside and tiger-striped appearance. It plays an important ecological role as a decomposer of dead hardwood in forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Saw-like Gills",
      "description": "The underside features sharp, blade-like teeth or 'gills' that resemble a saw, which is the origin of its common name",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Tiger-striped Cap",
      "description": "The tan to brown upper surface displays darker radial striations or bands that resemble tiger stripes",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Tough, Woody Texture",
      "description": "The fruiting body is rigid and woody, not soft or spongy like some other bracket fungi",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Tiger Sawgill grows on dead or dying hardwood logs and stumps throughout New England, preferring moist, shaded forest environments.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The sharp, tooth-like structures on the underside of the mushroom are technically called 'lamellae' and are much more rigid than true gills, allowing them to withstand the decomposition process over extended periods.",
